      "slug": "hi-vis-class-selector",
      "role": "spoke",
      "spokeType": "Interactive tool",
      "navLabel": "Hi-vis class selector",
      "cardTitle": "Hi-vis class selector",
      "cardBlurb": "Three questions → the class and colour your work points to, with the reasoning shown.",
      "crumbTail": "Tools",
      "kicker": "Interactive tool · Hi-vis",
      "h1": "Which hi-vis class do you need?",
      "metaTitle": "Hi-Vis Class Selector: Which Class Do You Need? | iNeedWorkwear",
      "metaDesc": "Answer three questions about where you work, the traffic around you and the light you work in — get the EN ISO 20471 class and colour your situation points to, with the reasoning.",
      "lede": "The class question has a logic to it: where you work, how fast the vehicles around you move, and how bad the light gets. Answer the three below and the tool walks the same decision path an assessor would — reasoning included.",
      "heroMeta": [ {"n":"3 questions","l":"Environment · traffic · light"}, {"n":"EN ISO 20471","l":"The logic behind it"}, {"n":"Not advice","l":"Your risk assessment decides"} ],
      "schema": { "type": "Article" },
      "blocks": [
        { "type":"prose", "variant":"alt", "klabel":"The tool", "h2":"Three questions",
          "html":"<div style=\"background:#fff;border:1.5px solid var(--line);border-left:4px solid var(--orange);border-radius:6px;padding:24px;max-width:680px\"><form id=\"hvq\" style=\"display:grid;gap:18px\"><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">1 · Where is the work?</legend><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"env\" value=\"rail\"> On or near the railway</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"env\" value=\"airport\"> Airside at an airport</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"env\" value=\"road\"> Roads, highways or verges</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"env\" value=\"site\"> Site, yard or depot with vehicles</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"env\" value=\"indoor\"> Indoors / away from moving vehicles</label></fieldset><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">2 · Fastest traffic near you</legend><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"spd\" value=\"fast\"> Over ~30 mph (open roads, highways)</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"spd\" value=\"slow\"> Slow, controlled (yard speed, FLTs, deliveries)</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"spd\" value=\"none\"> No vehicle movement where I work</label></fieldset><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">3 · Do you regularly work at night or in poor light?</legend><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 1.2rem .25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"lgt\" value=\"yes\"> Yes</label><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"lgt\" value=\"no\"> No</label></fieldset></form><div id=\"hvout\" style=\"margin-top:6px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:16px;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--slate)\"><em>Answer the questions above…</em></div></div><script>(function(){var f=document.getElementById('hvq');var out=document.getElementById('hvout');function v(n){var e=f.querySelector('input[name=\"'+n+'\"]:checked');return e?e.value:null}function res(title,body,links){out.innerHTML='<div style=\"font-family:var(--disp);font-size:1.5rem;color:#151B26;text-transform:uppercase;line-height:1.1\">'+title+'</div><p style=\"margin-top:8px\">'+body+'</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\">'+links+'</p><p style=\"margin-top:10px;font-size:.8rem\"><em>Guidance, not a risk assessment \\u2014 site rules and your employer\\u2019s assessment override this tool.</em></p>'}function go(){var env=v('env'),spd=v('spd'),lgt=v('lgt');if(!env){out.innerHTML='<em>Answer the questions above\\u2026</em>';return}if(env==='rail'){res('Orange Class 3 \\u2014 rail spec','Trackside work mandates orange garments to the rail spec (RIS-3279-TOM), and most principal contractors require Class 3 uppers \\u2014 commonly with orange trousers. Yellow is non-compliant on or near the line, whatever its class.','<a href=\"rail-spec-workwear-explained.html\">The rail spec explained \\u2192</a>');return}if(env==='airport'){res('Operator\\u2019s rules \\u2014 usually Class 3','Airports set their own airside clothing policies (class, colour, badging). Class 3 is the common baseline on vehicle-heavy aprons \\u2014 confirm the operator\\u2019s current requirements before buying for a crew.','<a href=\"class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis.html\">Class 2 vs 3 logic \\u2192</a>');return}if(!spd||!lgt){out.innerHTML='<em>Two more clicks\\u2026</em>';return}if(env==='road'||spd==='fast'||lgt==='yes'&&spd!=='none'){res('Class 3','High-speed traffic and poor light both demand maximum visible area \\u2014 sleeves or trouser bands included. '+(lgt==='yes'?'Night work alone is enough to justify Class 3 regardless of speed.':'On high-speed roads, Class 3 is the standard call.'),'<a href=\"class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis.html\">Why Class 3 here \\u2192</a>');return}if(env==='site'&&spd==='slow'){res('Class 2','The everyday workhorse \\u2014 a Class 2 vest or polo covers controlled-speed yards, depots and deliveries in daylight. Step up to Class 3 if night shifts or faster traffic enter the picture.','<a href=\"class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis.html\">Class 2 vs 3 detail \\u2192</a>');return}if(env==='indoor'&&spd!=='none'){res('Class 1\\u20132','Indoors around forklifts and MHE, Class 1 meets the letter \\u2014 but most operations issue Class 2 vests because one simple everyone-past-the-door rule beats zone policing.','<a href=\"warehouse-logistics-workwear-guide.html\">The warehouse kit map \\u2192</a>');return}res('Hi-vis may not be required','With no vehicle movement and no poor-light exposure, EN ISO 20471 hi-vis may not be a requirement at all \\u2014 though many employers issue Class 1\\u20132 anyway for consistency. Check your site rules.','<a href=\"hi-vis-workwear-guide.html\">Start at the hi-vis hub \\u2192</a>')}f.addEventListener('change',go)})();</script><p style=\"margin-top:14px;font-size:.85rem\"><em>No JavaScript? The full decision logic is in the table below.</em></p>" },
        { "type":"table", "klabel":"The logic on paper", "h2":"The same decision, as a table", "lead":"This is exactly what the tool walks through.",
          "head":["Situation","Points to","Why"],
          "rows":[
            ["On or near the railway","<span class='code'>Orange Class 3 (rail spec)</span>","RIS-3279-TOM mandates orange; PCs commonly require Class 3 + trousers"],
            ["Airside at an airport","<span class='code'>Operator's rules — usually Class 3</span>","Each airport sets airside clothing policy"],
            ["Roads, highways, verges","<span class='code'>Class 3</span>","High-speed traffic needs maximum visible area"],
            ["Regular night / poor-light work","<span class='code'>Class 3</span>","Light conditions raise the class regardless of speed"],
            ["Yard or depot, controlled speeds, daylight","<span class='code'>Class 2</span>","The everyday vest-or-polo territory"],
            ["Indoors around MHE","<span class='code'>Class 1–2</span>","Class 2 usually issued for rule simplicity"],
            ["No vehicles, good light","<span class='code'>Possibly none</span>","Check site rules — many issue Class 1–2 anyway"]
          ] },
        { "type":"citebox", "html":"Want the reasoning in full? <a href='class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis.html'>Class 2 vs Class 3</a> settles the common case; <a href='yellow-vs-orange-hi-vis.html'>yellow vs orange</a> settles the colour; <a href='en-iso-20471-hi-vis-classes-explained.html'>EN ISO 20471 explained</a> covers the standard itself." },
        { "type":"clusternav" },
        { "type":"faq", "klabel":"Questions", "h2":"Selector FAQ" }
      ],
      "faq":[
        {"q":"Is this tool a substitute for a risk assessment?","a":"No — it walks the standard decision logic (environment, traffic speed, light) to give you a well-reasoned starting point. Your employer's risk assessment and the site's rules are the documents that decide, and they override the tool wherever they're stricter."},
        {"q":"Why does night work push the answer to Class 3?","a":"Poor light degrades the daytime (fluorescent) half of hi-vis performance, so the standard's logic leans on greater visible area and more retroreflective tape — which is what Class 3 provides. Night work around any vehicle movement is Class 3 territory."},
        {"q":"The tool says Class 2 but my site demands Class 3 — who's right?","a":"The site. Principal contractors and operators are free to set stricter requirements than the risk-based minimum, and many standardise on Class 3 for simplicity or winter practicality. The stricter rule always wins."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "boot-code-finder",
      "role": "spoke",
      "spokeType": "Interactive tool",
      "navLabel": "Boot S-code finder",
      "cardTitle": "Boot S-code finder",
      "cardBlurb": "Three questions about ground, water and screening → the EN ISO 20345 code to put on the order.",
      "crumbTail": "Tools",
      "kicker": "Interactive tool · Safety footwear",
      "h1": "Which safety boot code do you need?",
      "metaTitle": "Boot S-Code Finder: S1 to S7 in Three Questions | iNeedWorkwear",
      "metaDesc": "Answer three questions — how wet the work is, what's underfoot, whether you pass metal detectors — and get the EN ISO 20345 S-code to specify, with the reasoning shown.",
      "lede": "Every S-code is an answer to two questions: how wet is the work, and what's lying on the ground? Add one modern complication — security archways — and three clicks land on the code to put on the order.",
      "heroMeta": [ {"n":"3 questions","l":"Water · ground · screening"}, {"n":"SB → S7","l":"The possible answers"}, {"n":"200 J","l":"Every result, same cap"} ],
      "schema": { "type": "Article" },
      "blocks": [
        { "type":"prose", "variant":"alt", "klabel":"The tool", "h2":"Three questions",
          "html":"<div style=\"background:#fff;border:1.5px solid var(--line);border-left:4px solid var(--orange);border-radius:6px;padding:24px;max-width:680px\"><form id=\"btq\" style=\"display:grid;gap:18px\"><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">1 · How wet is the work?</legend><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"wet\" value=\"dry\"> Dry — indoor floors, warehouse, workshop</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"wet\" value=\"clean\"> Wet-cleaned floors — food, hygiene, washdown areas</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"wet\" value=\"ground\"> Outdoor ground — mud, rain, general site</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"wet\" value=\"soaked\"> Constantly wet outdoors — full shifts in weather</label><label style=\"display:block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"wet\" value=\"standing\"> Standing water, slurry or concrete pours</label></fieldset><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">2 · Could nails, screws or sharp debris be underfoot?</legend><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 1.2rem .25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"pen\" value=\"yes\"> Yes / possibly</label><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"pen\" value=\"no\"> No — controlled floors</label></fieldset><fieldset style=\"border:0;padding:0\"><legend style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate);margin-bottom:8px\">3 · Do you pass through metal detectors / security screening?</legend><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 1.2rem .25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"met\" value=\"yes\"> Yes</label><label style=\"display:inline-block;margin:.25rem 0\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"met\" value=\"no\"> No</label></fieldset></form><div id=\"btout\" style=\"margin-top:6px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:16px;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--slate)\"><em>Answer the questions above…</em></div></div><script>(function(){var f=document.getElementById('btq');var out=document.getElementById('btout');function v(n){var e=f.querySelector('input[name=\"'+n+'\"]:checked');return e?e.value:null}function go(){var wet=v('wet'),pen=v('pen'),met=v('met');if(!wet||!pen||!met){out.innerHTML='<em>Answer all three\\u2026</em>';return}var code,why;if(wet==='standing'){code=pen==='yes'?'S5':'S4';why='Standing water and pours need a waterproof-by-construction polymer boot \\u2014 a safety wellington. '+(pen==='yes'?'The midsole makes it S5, the groundworks standard.':'No puncture risk keeps it S4, the hygiene wellington.')}else if(wet==='soaked'){code=pen==='yes'?'S7':'S6';why='Full shifts in weather justify certified whole-boot waterproofing (the WR test). '+(pen==='yes'?'With debris underfoot, S7 \\u2014 effectively a waterproof S3.':'On cleaner ground, S6 \\u2014 a waterproof S2.')+' Older stock may be labelled S3 WR / S2 WR \\u2014 same thing.'}else if(wet==='ground'){code='S3';why='Outdoor ground means a water-resistant upper, cleated outsole and midsole together \\u2014 which is exactly what S3 bundles. It\\u2019s the general site default for a reason.'}else if(wet==='clean'){code=pen==='yes'?'S3':'S2';why=pen==='yes'?'Washdown areas with debris risk push past S2 to S3.':'Wet-cleaned floors need the water-resistant upper of S2; food and hygiene sites usually spec washable styles.'}else{code=pen==='yes'?'S1P':'S1';why=pen==='yes'?'Dry floors with fixings or swarf underfoot \\u2014 S1P adds the puncture midsole to the light indoor spec. The common warehouse call, often as a safety trainer.':'Dry, controlled floors \\u2014 S1 covers it: toe cap, antistatic, energy-absorbing heel, no weight you don\\u2019t need. Safety-trainer styles carry the same certification.'}var extra=met==='yes'?'<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>Screening add-on:</strong> spec it metal-free \\u2014 composite toe cap and a textile (PL/PS) midsole \\u2014 so archways don\\u2019t cost you boots-off at every crossing. <a href=\"composite-vs-steel-toe-caps.html\">Why composite \\u2192</a></p>':'';out.innerHTML='<div style=\"font-family:var(--disp);font-size:1.6rem;color:#151B26;text-transform:uppercase\">'+code+'</div><p style=\"margin-top:8px\">'+why+'</p>'+extra+'<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><a href=\"safety-boot-codes-explained.html\">See '+code+' in the full code table \\u2192</a> \\u00b7 <a href=\"https://www.ineedworkwear.com/collections/footwear\">Shop safety footwear</a></p><p style=\"margin-top:10px;font-size:.8rem\"><em>Guidance, not a risk assessment \\u2014 site rules override. Many sites simply mandate S3.</em></p>'}f.addEventListener('change',go)})();</script><p style=\"margin-top:14px;font-size:.85rem\"><em>No JavaScript? The decision table below walks the same logic.</em></p>" },
        { "type":"table", "klabel":"The logic on paper", "h2":"The same decision, as a table",
          "head":["Wet?","Debris underfoot?","Answer","Why"],
          "rows":[
            ["Dry indoor","No","<span class='code'>S1</span>","Light spec: cap, antistatic, cushioned heel"],
            ["Dry indoor","Yes","<span class='code'>S1P</span>","Adds the puncture midsole — the warehouse default"],
            ["Washdown floors","No","<span class='code'>S2</span>","Water-resistant upper for hygiene and food areas"],
            ["Outdoor ground","Either","<span class='code'>S3</span>","Upper + midsole + cleats — the site standard"],
            ["Constantly wet","No / Yes","<span class='code'>S6 / S7</span>","Certified whole-boot waterproofing (WR)"],
            ["Standing water","No / Yes","<span class='code'>S4 / S5</span>","Safety wellingtons — waterproof by construction"]
          ] },
        { "type":"citebox", "html":"Full detail behind every code: <a href='safety-boot-codes-explained.html'>S1–S7 explained</a> · cap material: <a href='composite-vs-steel-toe-caps.html'>composite vs steel</a> · making the pair last: <a href='safety-boot-care.html'>boot care</a>." },
        { "type":"clusternav" },
        { "type":"faq", "klabel":"Questions", "h2":"Finder FAQ" }
      ],
      "faq":[
        {"q":"Why does the tool never suggest SB?","a":"SB is the bare toe-cap minimum with none of the antistatic, heel-cushioning or closed-seat features that S1 adds — and the price difference is trivial. In practice there's no working environment where SB is the right specification over S1."},
        {"q":"What if my site just says 'S3 boots required'?","a":"Then S3 it is — site rules override risk-based logic, and many principal contractors mandate S3 with ankle support as a blanket policy. The tool is most useful where nobody has specified anything and someone has to decide."},
        {"q":"Are safety trainers a code of their own?","a":"No — a safety trainer is a style, not a rating. An S1P trainer carries exactly the same certified toe cap and midsole as an S1P boot. What you give up is ankle coverage and weather resistance, which is why trainers suit the dry-indoor answers."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "ppe-checklist-builder",
      "role": "spoke",
      "spokeType": "Interactive tool",
      "navLabel": "PPE checklist builder",
      "cardTitle": "PPE kit checklist builder",
      "cardBlurb": "Tick the hazards a job actually has — get a head-to-toe kit checklist with the standard behind every item.",
      "crumbTail": "Tools",
      "kicker": "Interactive tool · PPE checklist",
      "h1": "Build a PPE checklist from the hazards.",
      "metaTitle": "PPE Kit Checklist Builder | iNeedWorkwear",
      "metaDesc": "Tick the hazards a job involves — vehicles, wet, cold, dust, chemicals, noise, kneeling, hygiene — and get a head-to-toe kit checklist naming the standard behind each item.",
      "lede": "Kit lists usually start from a job title and argue from there. This tool starts where risk assessments start — the hazards — and assembles the head-to-toe list, with the standard named next to every item so nothing is on the list by vibes.",
      "heroMeta": [ {"n":"9 hazards","l":"Tick what applies"}, {"n":"Standards named","l":"Every item earns its place"}, {"n":"Not an RA","l":"Your risk assessment governs"} ],
      "schema": { "type": "Article" },
      "blocks": [
        { "type":"prose", "variant":"alt", "klabel":"The tool", "h2":"Tick the hazards",
          "html":"<div style=\"background:#fff;border:1.5px solid var(--line);border-left:4px solid var(--orange);border-radius:6px;padding:24px;max-width:680px\"><form id=\"pcq\" style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:10px\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"veh\"> Vehicles or plant moving</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"impact\"> Heavy items that can drop</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"wet\"> Outdoor weather / wet work</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"cold\"> Cold store or winter static work</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"dust\"> Dust from cutting or sanding</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"chem\"> Cleaning or process chemicals</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"noise\"> Loud tools or plant</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"knee\"> Kneeling work</label><label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"hz\" value=\"hyg\"> Food or hygiene zone</label></form><div id=\"pcout\" style=\"margin-top:16px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:16px;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--slate)\"><em>Tick at least one hazard…</em></div></div><script>(function(){var f=document.getElementById('pcq');var out=document.getElementById('pcout');var M={veh:['Hi-vis to EN ISO 20471 — class via the <a href=\"hi-vis-class-selector.html\">class selector</a>'],impact:['Safety footwear with 200 J toe cap — code via the <a href=\"boot-code-finder.html\">S-code finder</a>','Hard hat to <a href=\"en-397-helmets-explained.html\">EN 397</a> where drops come from height'],wet:['Waterproof jacket to <a href=\"en-343-waterproofs-explained.html\">EN 343</a> — both class numbers healthy','Wicking <a href=\"base-layers-guide.html\">base layer</a> — never cotton next to skin'],cold:['Cold kit to <a href=\"en-342-cold-protection-explained.html\">EN 342</a> below −5 °C; EN 14058 for chill','Winter gloves rated under <a href=\"en-511-winter-gloves-explained.html\">EN 511</a> — water digit first outdoors'],dust:['FFP mask graded to the dust — <a href=\"en-149-ffp-ratings-explained.html\">EN 149</a>; FFP3 for silica','Sealed eye protection — <a href=\"en-166-eye-protection-explained.html\">EN 166</a> fields 4/5 for fine dust'],chem:['Chemical gloves to EN ISO 374 — chosen from the substance\\u2019s safety data sheet','Splash protection — <a href=\"en-13034-chem-splash-explained.html\">EN 13034 Type 6</a> for incidental splash only','Eye protection with liquid field 3 — <a href=\"en-166-eye-protection-explained.html\">EN 166</a>'],noise:['Hearing protection — defenders or plugs per the noise assessment'],knee:['Trousers with <a href=\"work-trousers-guide.html\">EN 14404 knee-pad pockets</a> — pads fitted, spares ordered'],hyg:['Washable slip-rated footwear — <a href=\"en-iso-20347-explained.html\">O2/S2 by handling risk</a>','Hairnets, aprons and gloves per the hygiene policy — <a href=\"disposable-ppe-guide.html\">the disposables guide</a>']};var base=['Gloves matched to the handling — <a href=\"en-388-glove-ratings-decoder.html\">EN 388 decoder</a>'];function go(){var c=f.querySelectorAll('input:checked');if(!c.length){out.innerHTML='<em>Tick at least one hazard…</em>';return}var items=[];c.forEach(function(b){(M[b.value]||[]).forEach(function(i){if(items.indexOf(i)<0)items.push(i)})});base.forEach(function(i){if(items.indexOf(i)<0)items.push(i)});out.innerHTML='<div style=\"font-family:var(--disp);font-size:1.3rem;color:#151B26;text-transform:uppercase\">The checklist</div><ul style=\"margin:.6rem 0 0 1.1rem\">'+items.map(function(i){return '<li style=\"margin-bottom:.45rem\">'+i+'</li>'}).join('')+'</ul><p style=\"margin-top:10px;font-size:.8rem\"><em>A starting checklist, not a risk assessment — your assessment and site rules govern, and PPE is the last control after safer ways of working.</em></p>'}f.addEventListener('change',go)})();</script><p style=\"margin-top:14px;font-size:.85rem\"><em>No JavaScript? Every mapping the tool knows is in the table below.</em></p>" },
        { "type":"table", "klabel":"The logic on paper", "h2":"Hazard → kit, as a table",
          "head":["Hazard ticked","Adds to the list","The standard"],
          "rows":[
            ["Vehicles or plant","Hi-vis, class by environment","<span class='code'>EN ISO 20471</span>"],
            ["Dropped-object risk","Toe-cap footwear; hard hat if from height","<span class='code'>EN ISO 20345 · EN 397</span>"],
            ["Weather / wet work","Rated waterproof + wicking base layer","<span class='code'>EN 343</span>"],
            ["Cold","Rated cold kit and winter gloves","<span class='code'>EN 342 · EN 511</span>"],
            ["Cutting dust","Graded FFP mask + sealed eyewear","<span class='code'>EN 149 · EN 166</span>"],
            ["Chemicals","SDS-matched gloves, splash kit, sealed eyewear","<span class='code'>EN ISO 374 · EN 13034 · EN 166</span>"],
            ["Noise","Hearing protection per assessment","Noise regs"],
            ["Kneeling","Knee-pad trousers with pads","<span class='code'>EN 14404</span>"],
            ["Hygiene zone","Washable slip-rated footwear, disposables","<span class='code'>EN ISO 20347</span>"]
          ] },
        { "type":"citebox", "html":"Every item links to its explainer — and the employer-side duties behind the list live in <a href='ppe-regulations-2022-explained.html'>the PPE regulations guide</a>." },
        { "type":"clusternav" },
        { "type":"faq", "klabel":"Questions", "h2":"Checklist FAQ" }
      ],
      "faq":[
        {"q":"Is this checklist a risk assessment?","a":"No — it's the kit consequence of hazards you already know about, with the right standard named per item. The risk assessment itself identifies and prioritises hazards, considers controls before PPE, and governs what's actually required."},
        {"q":"Why does every item name a standard?","a":"Because 'gloves' isn't a specification — EN 388 cut level C is. Naming the standard turns a checklist into an orderable list and stops the classic failure of buying kit that pattern-matches the hazard without actually being rated for it."},
        {"q":"What if my job's hazard isn't listed?","a":"The tool covers the nine most common workwear-relevant hazards. Specialist risks — arc flash, chainsaw work, molten metal, work at height — have their own guides on this site and their own certified kit; start from the standards hub or ask us."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
